 
          <p>In my <a href="/post/2026/08/a-beginners-guide-to-writing-snail-mail/">beginners guide to writing snail mail</a>, I hand out a few tips for getting started with letter writing as a lovely way of conversing with friends and strangers. I&rsquo;ve sent 21 letters since then, but the fun doesn&rsquo;t stop there: receiving a reply is just as exciting.</p>
<p>Many people dismiss letter writing as a thing of the past&mdash;an activity one engaged in when one still had the time to sit down and write. The more society sped up, the less letters are being written: just send a quick email instead. Or wait, no need to even open up your emailclient: just send a quick <em>instant</em> message using your <a href="/post/2025/07/how-to-bully-your-friends-into-using-signal/">favourite messaging platform</a> instead.</p>
<p>But as we turn towards digitised tools to communicate, we also implicitly accept the standardisation that comes with it: the symbols being translated into ones and zeroes can be styled using your client&rsquo;s font preferences, and sometimes we receive attaches, but that&rsquo;s about it. When you send or receive a letter, you send or receive a <em>filled canvas</em>. That work of art is the result of a burst of creativitiy, not the outcome of keystrokes on a keyboard. In other words, you are <strong>exchanging artworks</strong>. A few examples might help here:</p>
<ul>
<li>Handwriting, pen, and ink/colour combinations always result in a unique letter.</li>
<li>You can add stamps, paste stickers, <a href="/post/2021/09/hp-sprocket-mini-printer/">mini-printed photos</a>, washi tape pieces, &hellip; on there.</li>
<li>You can write in any angle, add postscripts, use a marker to highlight words or sentences, &hellip;</li>
<li>You can send along photocopied pages of books you&rsquo;re discussing.</li>
<li>You can cut out things and use that to decorate the letter, or use it as a basis of the letter.</li>
<li>You can send along tea bags that fit in an envelope for the receiver to enjoy. Can you do that using email? Didn&rsquo;t think so.</li>
<li>You can decorate the envelope itself.</li>
<li>You can create the envelope yourself using original recycled material.</li>
<li>&hellip;</li>
</ul>
<p>One of the envelopes I received turned out to be a recycled map from Direkt Recycling. Once you open these, a portion of a map reveals itself, but there are also envelopes you can buy with these on the exterior, such as the following design available through shops such as <a href="https://www.pollypaper.de/collections/direktrecycling">Polly Paper</a>.</p>
<p>
<figure><a href="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/11/on-receiving-letters/landkaart.jpg" class="lbox">
		<img src="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/11/on-receiving-letters/landkaart.jpg" loading="lazy" title="DRP DL Landkarte 10 Design-Kuverts Direktrecycling." data-pagefind-index-attrs="title">
	</a>
	
		<figcaption>DRP DL Landkarte 10 Design-Kuverts Direktrecycling.</figcaption></figure>
</p>
<p>Finding such an envelope in your mailbox is much more exciting than hearing the new mail jingle coming from your computer.</p>
<p>The excitement doesn&rsquo;t stop there. Receiving letters also provides the opportunity to admire the postage stamp and postmarks created by the sorting offices to encode the address and postage date. As mentioned in my previous article, here in Belgium you can design your own postage stamp&mdash;or of course buy one of the themed sets to decorate the top right corner of the envelope. None of these things are hard requirements for letter writing: a generic stamp of our favourite monarch will do. Yet it makes receiving a letter all the more fun.</p>
<p>I keep a dedicated postage stamp page in my current notebook to collect the neat ones I received. Once that one is full, I&rsquo;ll make a note of the page number where a new stamp page has been made to create a linked list enabling quick hops between those special pages. Of course, exchanging multiple letters with the same person yields predicable stamps. It&rsquo;ll take 16 more before my <em>Super Mario Land 2</em> ones are out.</p>
<p>
<figure><a href="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/11/on-receiving-letters/postzegels-in-boekje.jpg" class="lbox">
		<img src="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/11/on-receiving-letters/postzegels-in-boekje.jpg" loading="lazy" title="Left: notes on a biography of Blaise Pascal. Right: a dedicated postage stamp page." data-pagefind-index-attrs="title">
	</a>
	
		<figcaption>Left: notes on a biography of Blaise Pascal. Right: a dedicated postage stamp page.</figcaption></figure>
</p>
<p>It&rsquo;s always a treat to receive a letter, but it&rsquo;s not just the letter itself that&rsquo;s the treat!</p>
<p>Some of those sorting office postmarks come with little decorations that I try to preserve as well, such as the post horn left of the German <em>Start Trek</em> stamp and the <em>Freedom 250</em> circle of stars one on the round USA one. I&rsquo;ve been told that in the inner philately circles these are called &ldquo;cancelling marks&rdquo; as they prevent you from reusing a stamp on the next envelope. Today I also learned that there&rsquo;s an entire club dedicated to not collecting stamps but these postmarks (USA&rsquo;s Post Mark Collector&rsquo;s Club or the PMCC) and <a href="https://www.postmarks.org/museum/">a museum preserving early marks</a>. I&rsquo;m very much a newbie when it comes to these things so I have no idea whether a PMCC-esque network exists in the EU. Please help me become a true philatelist!</p>
<p>One of my pen pals writes on an old school Olivetti typewriter. When my wife dug up her late father&rsquo;s carefully preserved typewriter, it turned out that it was the same model! The ink ribbon might need replacement but I&rsquo;ll save that for another post. Typing with ten fingers on that thing is next to impossible. After a few attempts to compile a letter with the typewriter I reverted to one of the trusty fountain pens. Too much backspace misery.</p>
<p>If you want to learn more about envelope sizes I recommend reading the story of <a href="https://spitalfieldslife.com/2013/07/25/terry-smith-envelope-cutter/">Terry Smith the Envelope Cutter</a> over at Spitalfields Life:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Envelopes, especially of the brown manila variety, are mostly mundane objects that people prefer not to think about too much. But, at Baddeley Brothers, they make the envelopes of luxury and the envelopes of pleasure, envelopes with gilt crests embossed upon the flap, envelopes with enticing windows to peer through and envelopes lined with deep-coloured tissue – envelopes to lose yourself in. This is envelope-making as an art form, and Terry Smith is the supreme master of it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>We need more lovely articles like that. I stumble upon these posts using <a href="https://marginalia-search.com/search?query=envelope+design">Marginalia Search</a> by the way, not using Google et al.Happy letter sending &amp; receiving! If you&rsquo;re still looking for a pen pal, be sure to drop me a (digital and then later hopefully analogue) note.</p>


          
